The PR replaces raw string-concatenated SQL with psycopg2's `SQL`/`Identifier` composition and bound parameters. All dynamic parts (db_name, db_username, db_pwd, and the config-derived username) are now either bound parameters (`%s`) or wrapped in `Identifier`, which properly quotes identifiers. The `check_db_or_user_exists` function now uses bound parameters. No attacker-influenced value reaches SQL text unquoted or unbound. This is a hardening change, not an injection.

{
  "findings": []
}

The section under review consists only of test files. The PR's actual change to these files is adding `@pytest.mark.xfail` decorators to tests that previously asserted the old SQL-injection-vulnerable behavior (a "syntax error at or near '-'" from unquoted identifiers). The PR's driver change (in `postgres_cluster_driver.py`, not part of this section) replaces string-format SQL with `psycopg2.sql.SQL(...).format(Identifier(...))`, which is the hardening.

The test code itself does not build or execute SQL — it sends HTTP requests and calls backend methods. The SQL injection that the tests reference already existed in the base driver code (unchanged by this section) and is being fixed, not introduced, by the PR. Per the rules, injection that already exists in unchanged code is not reported, and the changed lines here are only test assertions/decorators that do not construct SQL.

{
  "findings": []
}

